<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Tungri</h1><p class="st-meta">50 BCE – 400 CE (approx.) · capital Atuatuca Tungrorum</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">People of eastern Belgium who settled the former land of the Eburones and whose town Atuatuca Tungrorum became Tongeren.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After Caesar destroyed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eburones">Eburones</a> in 53 BCE, new groups settled the area between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> says the Tungri were the first Germani to cross the Rhine, and that the name Germani spread from them to all their kin.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Their chief town <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongeren">Atuatuca Tungrorum</a> was founded under Augustus and grew into a walled city on the road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavay">Bavay</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-cologne">Cologne</a>. Tungrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cohort">cohorts</a> served across the empire, most famously on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a> and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vindolanda">Vindolanda</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The region suffered Frankish raids from the 3rd century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongeren">Tongeren</a> was rebuilt with a smaller wall. In the 4th century it became the seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/servatius">Servatius</a>, whose see later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht">Maastricht</a> and then <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/prince-bishopric-of-liege">Liège</a>. Tongeren claims to be the oldest town in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Belgium</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tungri.jpg" alt="Tungri" width="320" height="313"><figcaption>Image: Moutschen · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Germanie-inferieure.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>50 BCE – 400 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Atuatuca Tungrorum</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=175&amp;focus=tungri" aria-label="Tungri on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4804 416 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tungri lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5124 672L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5124 672h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5124 672h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Atuatuca Tungrorum. <a href="/atlas?year=175&amp;focus=tungri">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

